  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places inverted trough over the \nwestern Dakotas, with high over eastern Manitoba into Ontario. \nUpper level analysis places high over the southern Mississippi \nValley with ridge through the Midwest. To the west, trough \nnoted over the west coast resulting in southwest flow over the \nNorthern Plains. Over our area, broad area of rainfall continues\nover much of central and western North Dakota.\n\nFor the rest of this afternoon into this evening, broad area of\nrain previously mentioned will continue to gradually track\ntowards the east-northeast. Towards the backside where rain had\ntapered off over southwest North Dakota, CAMs have been\nconsistently showing some convection developing, some of which\nis showing up on radar over western South Dakota. Over our \narea, this is right in line with a narrow instability corridor \nadvertised on the RAP of CAPE values pushing around 1500 J/Kg. \nSo, cant rule out a stronger cell producing some small hail over\nthis area, but believe the severe threat should remain limited.\n\nAs we go through the night precipitation will gradually exit \nthe area. With the low level moisture in place there was some \nconcern for fog developing over the west, but believe this \nshould be limited by the expected north-northwesterly winds, but\ndo expect low ceilings to develop and possibly a few patches of\nfog particularly in higher terrain.\n\nQuieter weather expected over the area on Saturday as ridge\nstarts to rebuild over the Rockies, though temperatures will\nremain on the cooler side of average. Flow remains\nwest/northwesterly on Sunday, but a wave moving through the flow\nmay produce some showers/storms late Sunday into the overnight\nhours. \n\nTemperatures warm to near average for Monday before a strong\nshort wave and affiliated cold front push into the area, which\nwill bring cooler temperatures and medium chances (about 40 to\n60 percent) for precipitation on Tuesday. Remainder of the \nwork week looks to remain a bit active with occasional low \nchances for precipitation.",
